From: =?UTF-8?q?Kamil=20Postro=C5=BCny?= <kamil@postrozny.pl>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:01:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] iOS: the hand-built HostFeatures sets SupportsAVX as well

CPUFeatures.cpp's FEX_IOS_HOST branch builds its HostFeatures and returns
there, so FEX::FetchHostFeatures and OverrideFeatures never run: FEX_HOSTFEATURES
gets read (the TSO config line prints it) and ignored, and SupportsAVX ships
false. The decoder takes the VEX tables only with SupportsAVX (Frontend.cpp:82),
so without it the VEX group stays the invalid table and a VEX opcode at a block's
entry decodes INVALID_INST: The Witcher 3's installed 4.x build ends 1 s after it
starts at its first prologue store, vmovaps [rsp+0x50], xmm6 (witcher3.exe rva
0x27e8fef), with exit 0xc000001d, and its SSE fallback is never reached. The
enableavx experiment showed the switch cannot help: the launch carried it and the
ml538 marker's HostFeatures moved 288 -> 292, and the same store still decoded
invalid.

Setting the flag for the ARM64EC host only instead takes the AVX128 tables
(VEXTableOps_AVX128 and VEXTableGroupOps_AVX128, whose AVX128_* handlers -
AVX128_VMOVAPS among them - are already in the tree): SupportsSVE256 stays false,
so nothing reaches the 256-bit SVE path and no SVE context save/restore is
claimed. The flag belongs to the ARM64EC host alone: this FEX_IOS_HOST branch is
compiled into both FEX modules, and upstream answers WOW64 with false in this
function's other branch ("AVX is unsupported for WOW64"; that context has no ymm
save/restore). The assignment keeps the Wow64 host at upstream's answer, so an
i386 guest decodes no VEX, reports no AVX and keeps its own transition paths -
the 32-bit module gains nothing here and stays on the code that has run. On the
ARM64EC host the context already carries the ymm high halves in V[16..31], so
both transition directions now move them there: host -> guest always through
ReconstructXMMRegisters, guest -> host when the request carries CONTEXT_XSTATE
with XSTATE_AVX. That host's guest CPUID reports AVX and AVX2 and its XSAVE
leaves grow, which is what the flag means.

Only 128-bit host code is reached, so nothing here turns on 256-bit vectors; the
high halves' round trip through the EC context still needs its own check before a
title's compatibility status moves on them.

Linear: PLA-451
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-27-boot-regression.md
          docs/plans/2026-10-27-witcher3-avx.md
Offered-upstream: no; the iOS host's hand-rolled feature list is Playport's
---
 Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp b/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
index 3a61b45..9b724ba 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
@@ -71,6 +71,18 @@ FEXCore::HostFeatures CPUFeatures::FetchHostFeatures(bool IsWine, FEXCore::HostF
   HostFeatures.SupportsSHA = true;
   HostFeatures.SupportsPMULL_128Bit = true;
   HostFeatures.SupportsFCMA = true;
+  /* Playport: AVX128 too. This branch returns before FEX::FetchHostFeatures, so
+   * OverrideFeatures never runs and FEX_HOSTFEATURES' enableavx cannot set the
+   * flag: the decoder takes the VEX tables only when SupportsAVX is set, and
+   * without it a VEX opcode is INVALID_INST — The Witcher 3's installed 4.x
+   * build faults 1 s in at its first vmovaps with 0xc000001d. SupportsSVE256
+   * stays false, so the decoder takes VEXTableOps_AVX128 and its AVX128_*
+   * handlers, already in the tree; no SVE state is claimed here. Upstream keeps
+   * WOW64's AVX off (the non-iOS branch below clears it for Wow64,
+   * since that context has no ymm save/restore), and this branch compiles into
+   * both FEX modules: only the ARM64EC host gets the flag and only its x86-64
+   * guests decode VEX; the Wow64 host keeps upstream's false. */
+  HostFeatures.SupportsAVX = HostType != FEXCore::HostFeatures::HostTypeEnum::Wow64;
   HostFeatures.SupportsFlagM = true;
   HostFeatures.SupportsFlagM2 = true;
   HostFeatures.SupportsAFP = true;
